Songsri Tangsripairoj - CC (ccsts@mucc.mahidol.ac.th) wrote:
: 	I up the NIS in my network. DG/UX is my master and DEC/OSF1 is 
: my slave. I also set up the security in ENHANCED mode. However I can not 
: login to DEC host by using the same UID and Password that I usually use 
: to login to DG/UX. 

One possible cause of this could be left over files from before you
went NIS.  If login finds something in the /tcb/files/auth/*
directories it will either not look to NIS or mess up in some other
way.  (I know it gets confused because I've done this.  Exactly what
mistake it's making is another issue).  Secondly, at least in dUnix,
NIS appears to get really upset if somebody is listed both locally and
in the NIS database.  I would have thought that it would just default
to one or the other, but experience indicates that it doesn't take
either.

One thing you may want to try is getting on to the client as root, and
finding out whether it's aware of the username you're trying to log on
as.  You can use ypcat to look at the NIS database, or something as
simple as trying to change a users password would let you know if the
client even knows about the user.

